    ('s6', r"""\textbf{Before any choice, ad pixels of every vendor group fired from Shopify's sandboxes, and most first sandboxed requests came before the banner was visible.} An ad pixel `fired' when a Shopify sandbox sent an ad request before any choice (Default load), as in Figs~3 and 4. Colours, one meaning each: brick, consent lost (the first sandboxed requests in (b); the region default that allows them in (d)); slate, the comparison (d); amber, the banner becoming visible; dark slate, Shopify's sandbox (c); black and grey, stores with and without a banner (a, e), a contrast that is not about consent; other greys, context. (a)~Stores on which an ad pixel of each vendor group fired, among the <<fig3/a/any/banner/n>> stores that showed a banner (black circles) and the <<fig3/a/any/no_banner/n>> that did not (grey squares), each named on the `Any vendor' row, which is set apart below. Horizontal lines in (a) and (d): Wilson 95\% confidence intervals (CIs), in the colour of their mark. Keys in (c) to (e) sit above their plots. (b)~The <<fig3/b/n>> banner stores with a pre-choice sandboxed request: cumulative share whose first sandboxed ad request had been sent by time $t$, timed from the banner becoming visible (amber line; the time axis of Fig.~1b,c); thin light grey line: one polling interval of the banner detector (<<design/banner_poll_ms>>~ms) before the banner. The pale brick ground under the curve, left of the amber line, marks the stores that sent before the banner: the open ring where the curve meets the amber line is <<fig3/b/before_banner/k>> of <<fig3/b/n>> stores (<<fig3/b/before_banner/p|pct1>>\%), and the dot on the light grey line the <<fig3/b/before_banner_by_poll/k>> (<<fig3/b/before_banner_by_poll/p|pct1>>\%) that sent more than one poll before it. The <<fig3/b/n_gap_beyond_axis>> stores whose first request lies beyond the axis are counted at its ends, so the curve starts above the bottom of the axis and ends below its top; where it leaves the axis it ends in an arrowhead. In the <<fig3/b/repeat/n>> such stores loaded twice, `request first' agreed in <<fig3/b/repeat/agree>> ($\kappa$ <<fig3/b/repeat/kappa|f2>>). (c)~Origin of all ad requests in the Default load, by vendor group: Shopify sandbox (dark slate, the colour of the sandbox frame in Fig.~1a; web worker or sandboxed iframe, including frames nested in it), Shopify's open pixel (pixel type `OPEN', which runs in the page; mid grey) and ordinary page scripts and frames (light grey); thin white gaps separate the segments. Requests relayed from a sandbox by Shopify code in the page are Google's only (<<fig3/c/Google/relay_k>> of <<fig3/c/Google/n_requests>>; <<fig3/c/Google/relay|pct1>>\%): they keep their share of the bar but are narrower than a segment gap and are not drawn. Sandboxed share of each group's ad requests: Google <<fig3/c/Google/sandbox|pct1>>\% (<<fig3/c/Google/sandbox_k>> of <<fig3/c/Google/n_requests>>), TikTok <<fig3/c/TikTok/sandbox|pct1>>\% (<<fig3/c/TikTok/sandbox_k>> of <<fig3/c/TikTok/n_requests>>), Meta <<fig3/c/Meta/sandbox|pct1>>\% (<<fig3/c/Meta/sandbox_k>> of <<fig3/c/Meta/n_requests>>), Pinterest <<fig3/c/Pinterest/sandbox|pct1>>\% (<<fig3/c/Pinterest/sandbox_k>> of <<fig3/c/Pinterest/n_requests>>), Snap <<fig3/c/Snap/sandbox|pct1>>\% (<<fig3/c/Snap/sandbox_k>> of <<fig3/c/Snap/n_requests>>), Microsoft <<fig3/c/Microsoft/sandbox|pct1>>\% (<<fig3/c/Microsoft/sandbox_k>> of <<fig3/c/Microsoft/n_requests>>) and Reddit/other <<fig3/c/Reddit~1other/sandbox|pct1>>\% (<<fig3/c/Reddit~1other/sandbox_k>> of <<fig3/c/Reddit~1other/n_requests>>); across all vendors <<fig3/c/share_sandbox_boot/p|pct1>>\% (store-bootstrap 95\% CI <<fig3/c/share_sandbox_boot/lo|pct1>>--<<fig3/c/share_sandbox_boot/hi|pct1>>) of <<fig3/c/share_sandbox_boot/n>> requests were sandboxed. (d)~Banner stores on which any ad pixel fired, within five consent products, by Shopify's region default: `allowed until choice' (brick circles) and `denied until consent' (slate squares). These are the shares of Fig.~3a's four product rows, plus Shopify's banner, which Fig.~3a lists in its caption but does not draw, here under Fig.~3a's axis title with the stores under each default: Pandectes <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/pandectes/allowed/n>> and <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/pandectes/denied/n>>; OneTrust <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/onetrust/allowed/n>> and <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/onetrust/denied/n>>; Cookiebot <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/cookiebot/allowed/n>> and <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/cookiebot/denied/n>>; Osano <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/osano/allowed/n>> and <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/osano/denied/n>>; Shopify's banner <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/shopify_native/allowed/n>> and <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/by_product/shopify_native/denied/n>>; grey marks: fewer than <<design/min_stores_row>> stores under one default (Cookiebot `denied until consent', both of Osano's, whose name is grey, and Shopify's banner `allowed until choice'). The pooled Mantel--Haenszel difference over <<fig3/d/mh_by_product/n_strata>> products is in Fig.~3a (right). (e)~How many sandboxed ad requests each store sent before any choice (Default load, no click): share of stores with at least $n$ requests (complementary cumulative distribution; logarithmic $n$ axis), for the stores that showed a consent banner (black) and those that showed none (grey). Marks at the left end, at least one request: <<fig1b/banner_fires/k>> of <<fig1b/banner_fires/n>> banner stores and <<fig1b/nobanner_fires/k>> of <<fig1b/nobanner_fires/n>> stores without a banner; Wilson 95\% CIs <<fig1b/banner_fires/lo|pct1>>--<<fig1b/banner_fires/hi|pct1>>\% and <<fig1b/nobanner_fires/lo|pct1>>--<<fig1b/nobanner_fires/hi|pct1>>\%; a cluster bootstrap by consent tool widens them to <<fig1b/banner_fires/cb_lo|pct1>>--<<fig1b/banner_fires/cb_hi|pct1>>\% and <<fig1b/nobanner_fires/cb_lo|pct1>>--<<fig1b/nobanner_fires/cb_hi|pct1>>\%."""),
    ('s7', r"""\textbf{Blocked on paper in the main sample: vendors that a blocklist named still fired from Shopify's sandbox in every subset, while the blocker acted only on the page.} Colours: brick, sent before any choice (consent lost); slate, the comparison route (a page script); greys, context. All counts are for the main sample (Tranco top <<design/tranco_top_n>> ranks); the expansion to ranks 200k--1M and the combined estimate (primary set: <<round4/blocklist/combined/primary/fired/k>> of <<round4/blocklist/combined/primary/fired/n>> stores) are in Fig.~4a. (a)~Blocklist stores on which the vendor that the consent-management platform's (CMP's) blocklist declared blocked fired: its own sandboxed Shopify pixel sent an ad request before any choice (Default load). Top, set apart, the primary estimate (filled brick circle): stores whose CMP wrote no `marketing: yes', <<fig4/a/blocklist/primary/fired/p|pct1>>\% (<<fig4/a/blocklist/primary/fired/k>> of <<fig4/a/blocklist/primary/fired/n>>). Below, the sensitivity subsets (open brick circles): all <<fig4/a/blocklist/declared_vendor_fired_sbx/n>> blocklist stores (<<fig4/a/blocklist/declared_vendor_fired_sbx/k>> fired); the stores with the vendor's sandboxed pixel configured (<<fig4/a/blocklist/cfg_stores/fired/k>> of <<fig4/a/blocklist/cfg_stores/fired/n>>); without the <<fig4/a/blocklist/sens_excl_auto_grant/n_excluded>> stores whose CMP wrote `yes' by itself (<<fig4/a/blocklist/bot_rule/n>> of them because Pandectes' bot rule detected our automated browser; <<fig4/a/blocklist/sens_excl_auto_grant/fired/k>> of <<fig4/a/blocklist/sens_excl_auto_grant/fired/n>>); and without the <<fig4/a/blocklist/sens_excl_yes_before_pixel/n_excluded>> stores with a `yes' before the pixel started (<<fig4/a/blocklist/sens_excl_yes_before_pixel/fired/k>> of <<fig4/a/blocklist/sens_excl_yes_before_pixel/fired/n>>). Horizontal lines: Wilson 95\% confidence intervals (CIs). (b)~The blocklist entries on the <<d:s7b_routes/n_blocklist_stores>> blocklist stores. Left: the <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/n>> entries whose vendor has a configured sandboxed Shopify pixel (<<d:s7b_routes/cfg/n_stores>> stores) as a 2~$\times$~2 matrix of counts: whether the vendor sent an ad request before any choice from Shopify's sandbox (rows) and, counted independently, from an ordinary page script (columns); both routes <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/both>>; the sandbox only <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/sandbox_only>> (pale brick cell: consent lost only through the sandbox; the cells are split by two crossing rules, as in Fig.~S4a--c); a page script only <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/page_only>>; neither <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/neither>>. Right: share of entries sent before any choice, with Wilson 95\% CIs: the same entries from the sandbox (brick circle; <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/sandbox>> of <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/n>>) and from a page script (slate square; <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/page>> of <<d:s7b_routes/cfg/n>>) and, set apart, the <<d:s7b_routes/nocfg/n>> entries without an identified configured sandboxed pixel (<<d:s7b_routes/nocfg/n_stores>> stores; classic tag, open pixel or unknown installation) by any route (grey circle; <<d:s7b_routes/nocfg/any>> of <<d:s7b_routes/nocfg/n>>). (c)~One re-check load per blocklist store, after the main crawl, drawn as the ledger of Fig.~2a (each box: the stage, left, and its number of stores, right; the stores that leave a stage branch right, with their reason in grey, so that each count is the one above minus its branch; the brick arrow marks the leak). Of the <<d:s7c_recheck_nesting/stores>> blocklist stores the blocker was active in <<d:s7c_recheck_nesting/active>>; on <<d:s7c_recheck_nesting/declared_tag_neutralized>> it neutralised the tag of a vendor it declared blocked; on <<d:s7c_recheck_nesting/neutralized_and_sbx>> of these that vendor's sandboxed pixel still sent an ad request. (d)~The primary set store by store: its <<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/n>> blocklist entries whose vendor has a configured sandboxed Shopify pixel, on <<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/n_stores>> stores, as two store $\times$ vendor-group matrices with one layout, each on the ground of its region in Fig.~1a. Left, on the darker grey ground of the pixel sandboxes: the route through Shopify's sandbox (<<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/sandbox>> of <<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/n>> entries sent an ad request before any choice); right, on the light grey ground of the page: the route through an ordinary page script (<<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/page>> of <<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/n>>). Columns are the stores, grouped by the consent tool whose blocklist declared the vendor blocked (named below a rule under its columns); rows are the vendor groups used in every other figure (the one Reddit/other entry is Amazon Ads); brick: sent by that route; grey: not sent by that route (key above the right matrix); blank: no entry. The same position in both matrices is the same entry: <<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/both>> entries were sent by both routes and <<v2:fig4a_routes/primary_no_yes/neither_route>> by neither. Blocklists are readable for Pandectes, OneTrust and Cookiebot only."""),
    ('s8', r"""\textbf{After a choice, new sandboxes kept sending ad requests, and where a consent tool wrote `no' without a click a sandbox had almost always started first.} Colours: brick, consent lost; slate, the comparison; amber, the consent tool's write; greys, context. Every key sits above its plot. (a)~The per-vendor estimates of Fig.~5a, repeated here (they are not new estimates) as rows on the scale of (b), so that the vendors can be read against the subgroups of (b): on the <<fig5/a/n_clicked>> stores where the Decline click worked, stores on which a new sandbox sent an ad request to each vendor group under Default (filled black circles), on the fresh page after Decline (open squares; new sandboxes, <<design/equal_window_s>>~s) and under Global Privacy Control (GPC; filled grey triangles; first <<design/equal_window_s>>~s); `Any vendor' is set apart below. Lines in (a) and (b): Wilson 95\% confidence intervals (CIs), in the colour of their mark. (b)~The fresh-page measure of (a), any vendor (open squares), by banner type (top three rows; each grouping is named in grey above its rows): Shopify's own banner <<fig5/b/Shopify native banner/decline_residual/k>> of <<fig5/b/Shopify native banner/decline_residual/n>>; a consent-management platform (CMP) that writes to Shopify <<fig5/b/CMP writes to Shopify API/decline_residual/k>> of <<fig5/b/CMP writes to Shopify API/decline_residual/n>>; a blocker only, unknown or none <<fig5/b/Blocker only ~1 unknown ~1 none/decline_residual/k>> of <<fig5/b/Blocker only ~1 unknown ~1 none/decline_residual/n>>; and by the state Shopify recorded after the click (bottom three rows): explicit refusal <<fig5/b/Shopify recorded a refusal/decline_residual/k>> of <<fig5/b/Shopify recorded a refusal/decline_residual/n>>; `marketing' still allowed <<fig5/b/marketing still allowed/decline_residual/k>> of <<fig5/b/marketing still allowed/decline_residual/n>>; no record <<fig5/b/unknown ~1 no record/decline_residual/k>> of <<fig5/b/unknown ~1 no record/decline_residual/n>>. (c)~What the pixels that still sent requests declare, by window after a Decline (same page after the click; unload beacon at the reload; fresh page) among the <<fig5/c/decline_n>> stores where Shopify recorded a refusal, and under GPC among the <<fig5/c/gpc_n>> stores where Shopify set `sale of data: no'. Share of the window's stores whose still-sending pixel declares a purpose the visitor refused (brick), only purposes the choice left allowed (grey) or no purpose (dark grey), in the class names, colours, key order and bar form of Fig.~5b: one thin bar per class from zero, side by side rather than stacked because a store can be in several classes, with its Wilson 95\% CI as a dark line, as in Fig.~5b; a class with no store has no bar, only its interval. Stores whose pixel declares a refused purpose: <<fig5/c/composition/decline/same_page/refused/k>> (same page), <<fig5/c/composition/decline/unload/refused/k>> (unload beacon), <<fig5/c/composition/decline/fresh_page/refused/k>> (fresh page) and <<fig5/c/composition/gpc/refused/k>> (GPC); only purposes left allowed: <<fig5/c/composition/decline/same_page/left_allowed/k>> (same page), <<fig5/c/composition/decline/unload/left_allowed/k>> (unload beacon), <<fig5/c/composition/decline/fresh_page/left_allowed/k>> (fresh page) and <<fig5/c/composition/gpc/left_allowed/k>> (GPC); no purpose: <<fig5/c/composition/decline/same_page/none/k>> (same page), <<fig5/c/composition/decline/unload/none/k>> (unload beacon), <<fig5/c/composition/decline/fresh_page/none/k>> (fresh page) and <<fig5/c/composition/gpc/none/k>> (GPC). A store can appear in several classes and windows (one class per store: Fig.~5b). (d)~The race in Fig.~5c's raster and time axis: one row per page load in which a CMP wrote `marketing: no' or `yes' without a click, in four blocks (`no' in the Default load, <<d:s8d_race_loads/deny_A0/loads>> loads; `no' under GPC, <<d:s8d_race_loads/deny_A2/loads>>; `yes' in the Default load, <<d:s8d_race_loads/grant_A0/loads>>; `yes' under GPC, <<d:s8d_race_loads/grant_A2/loads>>), each block sorted by its loads' first third-party sandbox that declares `marketing'. One tick per load, at that sandbox's start minus the CMP's consent write (amber line, named above the plot), on Fig.~5c's symmetric-log axis (linear close to the write, logarithmic beyond; the two innermost tick labels of Fig.~5c are omitted here). Brick: the sandbox started before the `no'; grey: after the `no'; slate: after a `yes'. A wider gap sets the two `yes' blocks apart from the two `no' blocks. The pale brick ground between the brick ticks and the amber line marks the time each of those sandboxes ran before the `no'. In <<fig5/d/deny/loads_first_before>> of <<fig5/d/deny/loads>> `no' loads a sandbox started first (the other <<d:s8d_race_loads/deny_A2/first_after>> are GPC loads); in all <<fig5/d/grant/loads>> `yes' loads it started after. Stores where a CMP wrote `marketing: no' without a click and a sandboxed ad request was still sent before any choice: <<fig4/a/cpapi_deny/any_sbx/k>> of <<fig4/a/cpapi_deny/any_sbx/n>> (<<fig4/a/cpapi_deny/any_sbx/p|pct1>>\%; Wilson 95\% CI <<fig4/a/cpapi_deny/any_sbx/lo|pct1>>--<<fig4/a/cpapi_deny/any_sbx/hi|pct1>>\%). The per-sandbox version is Fig.~5c."""),
    ('s9', r"""\textbf{Vendors received the full landing URL in every load, refusal signals mostly in Google's requests, and a creator's per-video code on every load of Store C.} Colours: brick, requests that carried the per-video code; slate, Google beacons whose Consent Mode state denied ad storage; amber, the banner becoming visible and the Decline click; greys, context. (a)~Sandboxed ad beacons carrying the full landing URL with our test code, by vendor group and load: Default (filled black circles), Decline (open squares) and Global Privacy Control (GPC; filled grey triangles); key above the plot; Korn--Graubard 95\% confidence intervals (CIs) clustered by store. Stores per load (Default / Decline / GPC): Google <<fig6/a/Google/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Google/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Google/GPC/n_stores>>; TikTok <<fig6/a/TikTok/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/TikTok/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/TikTok/GPC/n_stores>>; Meta <<fig6/a/Meta/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Meta/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Meta/GPC/n_stores>>; Pinterest <<fig6/a/Pinterest/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Pinterest/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Pinterest/GPC/n_stores>>; Snap <<fig6/a/Snap/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Snap/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Snap/GPC/n_stores>>; Microsoft <<fig6/a/Microsoft/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Microsoft/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Microsoft/GPC/n_stores>>; Reddit/other <<fig6/a/Reddit~1other/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Reddit~1other/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/Reddit~1other/GPC/n_stores>>; all vendors <<fig6/a/All/Default/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/All/Decline/n_stores>> / <<fig6/a/All/GPC/n_stores>>. A load with fewer than <<design/min_stores_row>> stores for a vendor (Meta and Snap under GPC) is not drawn. Markers in (b) and (c) as in (a). (b)~One plot on the scale of (a), in two groupings named in grey above their rows. `Ad requests': ad requests carrying a refusal signal in the URL (Google: Consent Mode, `npa', `rdp' or `us\_privacy'; Microsoft: `asc=D'), in the Default load, on the next page after Decline and in the GPC load; 95\% CIs from resampling stores (percentile bootstrap, <<design/bootstrap_B>> resamples). On the fresh page after Decline (open squares): Google <<fig6/c/Google/Decline/k>> of <<fig6/c/Google/Decline/n>> (<<fig6/c/Google/Decline/p|pct1>>\%), Microsoft <<fig6/c/Microsoft/Decline/k>> of <<fig6/c/Microsoft/Decline/n>> (<<fig6/c/Microsoft/Decline/p|pct1>>\%), all other vendors <<fig6/c/Other vendors/Decline/k>> of <<fig6/c/Other vendors/Decline/n>>. `Stores, all flagged': stores still sending sandboxed ad requests on which every one of them carried a refusal signal (open square: the fresh page after Decline; grey triangle: GPC): <<fig5/flags/resid_decline_fresh/stores_all_flagged>> of <<fig5/flags/resid_decline_fresh/stores>> on the fresh page after Decline and <<fig5/flags/resid_gpc/stores_all_flagged>> of <<fig5/flags/resid_gpc/stores>> under GPC (Wilson 95\% CIs). (c)~Google's ad requests only. Left: beacons by the Consent Mode state in their `gcs' parameter: ad storage denied (G100 or G101; slate), granted (G111; dark grey) or no state (light grey); thin white gaps separate the segments; key above the plot. Beacons: <<d:s9b_gcs_states/Default/n>> in the Default load, <<d:s9b_gcs_states/Decline_next/n>> on the fresh page after Decline and <<d:s9b_gcs_states/GPC/n>> in the GPC load; other states (at most <<d:s9c_other_state/max_share|pct1>>\% of a load's beacons) are too few to draw. Right: share of Google's ad requests carrying each signal (`gcs' or `gcd' with ad storage denied, `npa=1', `rdp=1', a `us\_privacy' opt-out; markers as in (a)). These are request-level shares of the same requests (Default <<d:s9c_google_flag_shares/Default/n>>; Decline <<d:s9c_google_flag_shares/Decline_next/n>>; GPC <<d:s9c_google_flag_shares/GPC/n>>); because requests cluster within stores, their 95\% CIs come from resampling stores, as in (b); an interval narrower than its marker is hidden behind it. (d)~Store~C, a creator-linked store, reached from the creator's video link: every ad request in five loads (Default and its repeat, Decline, GPC and its repeat) as an event strip on one broken time axis with one scale. The axis is cut (double slashes) where nothing was sent or happened for a long span, and two short, sparse stretches share the middle stretch. Each load has a lane of requests from a sandbox (`Sandbox', on the darker grey ground of Fig.~1a's pixel sandboxes) and a lane of all others (`Other', on the light grey ground of the page). One tick per group of requests of one vendor group and kind less than <<d:s9d_tick_groups/merge_ms>>~ms apart, at their mid time (<<d:s9d_tick_groups/n_groups_multi>> ticks stand for more than one request); tall ticks: data beacons and other requests, brick when at least one request of the tick carried the per-video code and grey when none did; short light grey ticks: library loads, none of which carried the code (key above the plot). Amber lines: the banner becoming visible and the Decline click; grey line: the reload in the Decline load; each event is named above its own line, the banner once. In the sandbox lanes of the Default, repeat and Decline loads the first two ticks are Google's, the two short light grey ticks are TikTok's library loads and the last tick stands for the <<d:s9d_tick_groups/example/n>> code-carrying TikTok requests counted in the caption of Fig.~6a; nothing was sent from a sandbox in the GPC loads. The code reached <<fig6/b/store_c/A0/n_vendors_with_token>> vendors (<<fig6/b/store_c/A0/n_vendors_with_token_nongoogle>> other than Google) in the Default load and <<fig6/b/store_c/A0r/n_vendors_with_token>> (<<fig6/b/store_c/A0r/n_vendors_with_token_nongoogle>>) in its repeat; in the Decline load <<fig6/b/store_c_pre_click/n_vendors>> before the click and <<fig6/b/store_c_after_decline/n_vendors>> after the reload (<<fig6/b/store_c_after_decline/n_vendors_sandboxed>> from a sandbox); under GPC <<fig6/b/store_c/A2/n_vendors_with_token>> (<<fig6/b/store_c/A2/n_vendors_with_token_nongoogle>>) and <<fig6/b/store_c/A2r/n_vendors_with_token>> (<<fig6/b/store_c/A2r/n_vendors_with_token_nongoogle>>) in its repeat. Signals carried in request bodies are not visible."""),
    ('s10', r"""\textbf{Beyond Shopify: in a pilot of selected Wix, BigCommerce and first-party-gateway sites, ad requests were common before any choice, after a working Decline and under Global Privacy Control (GPC).} The sites are a pilot of selected sites, not a sample: <<round4/beyond/funnel/wix/selected>> Wix and <<round4/beyond/funnel/bigcommerce/selected>> BigCommerce sites (<<round4/beyond/funnel/wix/captured>> and <<round4/beyond/funnel/bigcommerce/captured>> captured) and <<round4/beyond/funnel/gateway/selected>> sites whose Google tag loads through a first-party tag gateway or tagging server (`first-party gateway'; <<round4/beyond/funnel/gateway/captured>> captured). They were crawled on <<d:s10_crawl_date|raw>> from one California vantage point with the same three loads as the main study (Default, Decline, GPC). An ad pixel `fired' when the site sent an ad request: a request to an advertising vendor, or a first-party request whose wire format names Google Ads, Meta or TikTok (the axis titles of Figs~3 and 4). Forwarding onward from a server (server-side) is not observable and is never inferred. The three setups are the rows of every plot. (a)~Banner sites on which any ad pixel fired in the Default load, before any choice: Wix <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/prechoice_ad_banner/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/prechoice_ad_banner/n>>; BigCommerce <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/prechoice_ad_banner/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/prechoice_ad_banner/n>>; first-party gateway <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/prechoice_ad_banner/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/prechoice_ad_banner/n>>. (b)~Sites where the Decline click worked. Left: an ad pixel fired after the click, on the same page or the next page (Wix <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/decline/after_ad/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/decline/after_ad/n>>; BigCommerce <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/decline/after_ad/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/decline/after_ad/n>>; gateway <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/decline/after_ad/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/decline/after_ad/n>>). Right: a first-party collect beacon after the click, that is a GA4, Google Ads, Universal Analytics, Meta or TikTok beacon sent to the site's own domain or tagging server: Wix <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/decline/after_fp_collect/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/decline/after_fp_collect/n>>; BigCommerce <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/decline/after_fp_collect/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/decline/after_fp_collect/n>>; gateway <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/decline/after_fp_collect/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/decline/after_fp_collect/n>>. (c)~All sites with a Default and a GPC load: any ad pixel fired in each load, Default above GPC (Wix <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/gpc/A0_ad/k>> and <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/gpc/A2_ad/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/wix/gpc/A0_ad/n>>; BigCommerce <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/gpc/A0_ad/k>> and <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/gpc/A2_ad/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/bigcommerce/gpc/A0_ad/n>>; gateway <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/gpc/A0_ad/k>> and <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/gpc/A2_ad/k>> of <<round4/beyond/rates/gateway/gpc/A0_ad/n>>). Markers, in one key above (c): Default load (filled black circle), Decline load (open square), GPC load (filled grey triangle); lines: Wilson 95\% confidence intervals; light grey marks: fewer than <<design/min_stores_row>> sites (the gateway sites with a working Decline click, in (b)); every plot has the same scale. Two case studies give no rates and are not drawn. Partytown, which runs third-party scripts in a web worker: of <<round4/beyond/case_summary/partytown/selected>> selected sites, <<round4/beyond/case_summary/partytown/captured>> were captured; requests from Partytown workers numbered <<round4/beyond/case_summary/partytown/pt_worker_requests>> in all loads, and the <<round4/beyond/case_summary/partytown/sites_ad_default>> sites with an ad request in the Default load sent it from the top frame's main thread (<<round4/beyond/case_summary/partytown/sites_ad_main_thread_default>> sites) and from iframes (<<round4/beyond/case_summary/partytown/sites_ad_subframe_default>> site). Zaraz, which runs tools on Cloudflare's servers: on the <<round4/beyond/case_summary/zaraz/selected>> selected site Zaraz loaded, made <<round4/beyond/case_summary/zaraz/zaraz_track_calls>> track calls in all loads, and sent no ad request in any load. Rates describe the selected sites only: they are not population estimates, the setups are not compared statistically, and they are not comparable with the Shopify rates of the main study, which count ad requests from Shopify's sandbox."""),
